Here's a Bash script that downloads the JPEG files specified in the `after_rendition` column and names them `after-uuid.jpg`. It uses `curl` to download the files and `jq` to extract the UUID from the `xmp_camera_raw` field. The script also includes a rate limiter using `sleep` to ensure that no more than 10 files are downloaded per second.

```bash
#!/bin/bash

# Set the CSV file path
csv_file="path/to/your/csv_file.csv"

# Set the output directory
output_dir="path/to/output/directory"

# Set the rate limiter sleep time
rate_limiter_sleep=0.1

# Extract the UUID from the xmp_camera_raw field using jq
uuid_extractor='jq -r '.xmp_camera_raw | tostring | split("_")[1]'

# Loop through each row in the CSV file
while IFS=',' read -ra row; do
    # Extract the ID, before_rendition, after_rendition, xmp_camera_raw, dng, published, author_id, author_user_name, and author_url fields
    id=${row[0]}
    before_rendition=${row[1]}
    after_rendition=${row[2]}
    xmp_camera_raw=${row[3]}
    dng=${row[4]}
    published=${row[5]}
    author_id=${row[6]}
    author_user_name=${row[7]}
    author_url=${row[8]}

    # Extract the UUID from the xmp_camera_raw field
    uuid=$(echo "$xmp_camera_raw" | $uuid_extractor)

    # Construct the output file name
    output_file="${output_dir}/after-${uuid}.jpg"

    # Download the JPEG file using curl
    curl -o "${output_file}" "${after_rendition}"

    # Apply the rate limiter
    sleep ${rate_limiter_sleep}
done <(cat "${csv_file}")
```

