The CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) error in WordPress can be fixed for Next.js by adding the necessary headers to your server configuration. In Next.js, you can use the `next.config.js` file to configure the server settings.

Here's an example of how to add the necessary headers to your `next.config.js` file:
```javascript
module.exports = {
  // ...
  server: {
    headers: [
      { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin', value: '*' },
      { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Methods', value: 'GET, POST, PUT, DELETE' },
      { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Headers', value: 'Authorization, Content-Type' },
    ],
  },
  // ...
};
```
This will allow your Next.js application to make requests to any origin, using any method, and with any header. You can adjust the `value` property to restrict access as needed.

Alternatively, you can also use a middleware function to add the necessary headers to your responses. Here's an example of how to do this:
```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

app.use((req, res, next) => {
  res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Origin', '*');
  res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Methods', 'GET, POST, PUT, DELETE');
  res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Headers', 'Authorization, Content-Type');
  next();
});

app.use(require('./pages'));

app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server ready at http://localhost:3000');
});
```
This will have the same effect as the `headers` option in the `next.config.js` file, but it allows you to use a more fine-grained approach to configuring your server.

