To divide each hour into 10 equal parts in Excel, you can use the following formula:

`=TIME(HOUR(A1), (MINUTE(A1) / 6), 0)`

Assuming your data is in column A, starting from cell A1. This formula will return the first 10-minute interval of the hour in the format `HH:MM`. You can then drag this formula down to apply it to the rest of the hour's measurements.

In MATLAB, you can use the following code snippet to achieve the same result:

```matlab
% Assuming your data is in a vector called 'data'
num_hours = length(data);
interval_length = 6; % 10 minutes in seconds

for i = 1:num_hours
    hour = floor(data(i) / 3600);
    minute = mod(data(i), 3600) / 60;
    start_time = datetime(hour, minute, 0);

    for j = 1:10
        end_time = start_time + interval_length * timedelta('minute');
        % Perform analysis on data within this 10-minute interval
        disp(['Analysis for hour ', num2str(hour), ' minute ', num2str(minute), ' to ', num2str(end_time)]);
        start_time = end_time;
    end
end
```

This code will iterate through each hour in your data and divide it into 10 equal intervals of 10 minutes each. It will then perform some analysis within each interval. Replace the `disp` function with your desired analysis method.

To divide each hour into 10 equal parts, you can use the following formula in Excel:

`=TIME(HOUR(A1), MINUTE(A1)/6, SECOND(A1))`

This formula takes the current time (A1) and divides the minute by 6 to get the fraction of an hour. It then adds this fraction to the hour to get the new time in hours. Repeat this process for each hour in your dataset.

In MATLAB, you can use the following code:

```matlab
% Input data
data = load('your_dataset.mat');

% Extract hourly data
hours = data(:, 1);

% Divide each hour into 10 equal parts
new_hours = cell(length(hours), 1);
for i = 1:length(hours)
    new_hours{i} = datetime(hours{i}, 'Format', 'hh:mm:ss') + duration(0, 0, 0, 0, 6);
end

% Display the new hours
disp(new_hours);
```

This code assumes that your dataset is stored in a .mat file named "your_dataset.mat". It extracts the hourly data from the dataset and divides each hour into 10 equal parts using the `datetime` function. The resulting `new_hours` array contains the divided hours as datetime objects.

Here's a Go program that parses a struct in a Go source file and generates a Avro schema from it:

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"io/ioutil"
	"regexp"
	"strings"

	"github.com/apache/avro/v2/schema"
)

func main() {
	fileName := "example.go"
	structName := "MyStruct"
	outputFile := "struct.avsc"

	content, err := ioutil.ReadFile(fileName)
	if err != nil {
		fmt.Println("Error reading file:", err)
		return
	}

	re := regexp.MustCompile(`type (\w+) struct`)
	matches := re.FindAllStringSubmatch(string(content), -1)

	if len(matches) == 0 {
		fmt.Println("No struct found in the file.")
		return
	}

	structType := matches[0][1]
	fields := []schema.Field{}

	for _, match := range matches {
		fieldName := match[2]
		fieldType := match[3]

		fields = append(fields, schema.Field{
			Name:  fieldName,
			Type:  schema.ParseType(fieldType),
		})
	}

	schemaDef := schema.SchemaDef{
		Name:  structName,
		Fields: fields,
	}

	avroSchema, err := schema.FromDef(schemaDef)
	if err != nil {
		fmt.Println("Error generating Avro schema:", err)
		return
	}

	err = ioutil.WriteFile(outputFile, avroSchema.ToJSON(), 0644)
	if err != nil {
		fmt.Println("Error writing file:", err)
		return
	}

	fmt.Printf("Avro schema generated and written to %s\n", outputFile)
}
```

