Here's an example of a logical syllogism:

[Latex]
\begin{syllogism}
    \begin{premise}
        All humans are mortal.
    \end{premise}
    \begin{premise}
        Socrates is human.
    \end{premise}
    \begin{conclusion}
        Therefore, Socrates is mortal.
    \end{conclusion}
\end{syllogism}

This syllogism works by using two premises to conclude a statement about the subject (Socrates). The first premise states that all humans are mortal, which means that it applies to every single human being. The second premise states that Socrates is human, which means that he falls under the category of "human." Since the first premise applies to all humans, including Socrates, it follows that Socrates must also be mortal. This is a valid and sound argument because the premises provide sufficient information to reach the conclusion.

A syllogism is a type of argument consisting of two premises and a conclusion, where the conclusion logically follows from the premises. Here's an example of a logical syllogism:

Premise 1: All humans are mortal.
Premise 2: Socrates is human.
Conclusion: Socrates is mortal.

This syllogism works by applying the first premise to the second premise. The first premise states that all humans are mortal, which means that any being that is classified as human must also be mortal. Since Socrates is human, according to the second premise, we can conclude that he is also mortal, based on the first premise.

Here's the same syllogism formatted in LaTeX:

\begin{syllogism}
    \premise{All humans are mortal.}
    \premise{Socrates is human.}
    \conclusion{Socrates is mortal.}
\end{syllogism}

\begin{itemize}
    \item Premise 1: All humans are mortal. (A)
    \item Premise 2: Socrates is human. (B)
    \item Conclusion: Therefore, Socrates is mortal. (C)
\end{itemize}

This is a simple example of a logical syllogism in the form of a deductive argument. It follows the traditional structure of a syllogism with two premises and a conclusion. The first premise states that all humans are mortal, which means they will eventually die. The second premise identifies Socrates as a human. The conclusion then infers that Socrates must also be mortal because he is a human, and therefore subject to the same fate as all other humans. This argument is valid and sound, meaning it is logically correct and the premises support the conclusion.

