To check if all non-NA elements in a character vector can be converted to numeric, you can use the following R code:

```R
# Function to convert character vector to numeric and check for NA values
is_numeric_vector <- function(x) {
  tryCatch({
    as.numeric(x[!is.na(x)])
    TRUE
  }, error = function(e) FALSE)
}

# Example usage
x <- c("1", "2", "3", "4", NA, "5")
if (is_numeric_vector(x)) {
  print("All non-NA elements in x can be converted to numeric.")
} else {
  print("Not all non-NA elements in x can be converted to numeric.")
}
```

In this code, we define a function `is_numeric_vector` that takes a character vector `x` as input. The function first tries to convert the non-NA elements of `x` to numeric using `as.numeric`. If the conversion is successful, the function returns `TRUE`. If an error occurs during the conversion (e.g., if `x` contains non-numeric values or NA values), the function catches the error and returns `FALSE`.

We then demonstrate the use of the function by creating a character vector `x` that contains a mix of numeric and non-numeric values, including NA. We call the `is_numeric_vector` function with `x` as input and print the result. In this case, since not all non-NA elements in `x` can be converted to numeric, the function returns `FALSE`, and the output is "Not all non-NA elements in x can be converted to numeric."

